    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-112004",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-9x-18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & -9 & -18 \\\\  &  & -3 & 3 & 18 \\\\  & 1 & -1 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x+2)(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+3)(0+2)(0-3) \\\\  & =(3)(2)(-3) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-18,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250730/assets/c3d41c300747f4f0f18607351f2f03241c9a789ed70fe72e05c5a385b708c4c8.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
